                %li
                  %a{:href => "#response_from_toby"} Response from Toby
          %h2#attempt_2 Attempt #2
          %h3#definition_experimental Definition (Experimental)
          %h2#attempt_1 Attempt #1
          %h3#definition_experimental_2 Definition (Experimental)
          .standout
            %blockquote
              %p
                Note the
                %strong Crash!
                below. This definition is not correct. I think something like this should work, but I need to think about it.
          %p
            Given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:D\to C
            form an
            %strong adjunction
            if for every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:x\to y
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{y x}: G\circ F(x)\to y
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{x y}: G\circ F(y)\to x
          %p such that the following diagram commutes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi G
                              %mo ∘
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  x &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; y
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  G\circ F(y)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{G\circ F(f)}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  G\circ F(x)
                  }.
          %p
            Note that since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\circ F:C\to C
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x: x\to G\circ F(x)
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y: y\to G\circ F(y)
          %p such that the following diagram also commutes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi G
                              %mo ∘
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  x &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  G\circ F(x)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{G\circ F(f)}{\rightarrow}&amp;
                  G\circ F(y)
                  }.
          %p Putting the two commuting squares together, we have a commuting square with commuting (upward) diagonals giving rise to additional commuting triangles.
          %p These commuting diagrams give rise to the following relations:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mi x
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{y x}\circ \alpha_x = f
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi y
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi G
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y\circ\eta_{y x} = G\circ F(f)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi G
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi y
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [G\circ F(f)]\circ\alpha_x = \alpha_y\circ f
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi y
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi G
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{x y}\circ [G\circ F(f)]\circ\alpha_x = 1_x
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi y
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi y
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ\eta_{x y}\circ\alpha_y = 1_y.
          %p Note: I’m not sure if these are independent or not (need to count how many independent equations we get!), but it is instructive to note:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{x y}\circ \alpha_y\circ\eta_{y x}\circ\alpha_x = 1_x
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{y x}\circ\alpha_x\circ\eta_{x y}\circ \alpha_y = 1_y
          %p which means
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi y
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi y
                      %mo )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{y x}\circ\alpha_x = \left(\eta_{x y}\circ \alpha_y\right)^{-1}.
          %p
            %strong CRASH!
          %p I’ll need to check, but unless I made some algebra mistake, this last statement might kill the whole idea. Since
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mi x
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = \eta_{y x}\circ\alpha_x,
          %p
            then the above statement says
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            has an inverse.
          %h3#response_from_toby Response from Toby
          %p
            You need to say more than just that
            %em there are
            morphisms that make these diagrams commute. Whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \vdash G
            is an adjunction is not actually a property but a structure; we need to say
            %em how
            they are an adjunction. That is, you have to pick the family of morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(F(x)) \to y
            in advance.
          %p
            But then you can make it work, I believe … although I'm pretty sure that you need more commutative diagrams than the one that you've drawn. That is, an
            %strong adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            consists of a family of maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_f\colon G(F(x)) \to y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon x \to y
          %ul
            %li a bunch of diagrams commute.
          %p I have to think about whether this works and what diagrams you need.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Thanks Toby. I would be surprised if it were this simple, but I drew some pictures based on the diagram in Goldblatt, and I think this works.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Note, I had some typos in the original version of the diagram, but it should be correct now. Or it least it now matches the diagram on my paper :)
          %blockquote
            %p I'm pretty sure that you need more commutative diagrams than the one that you've drawn.
          %p
            We get another commuting square from the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\circ F
            is an endofunctor. I’ve now drawn that above. When you put the two together, it gives you a bunch of additional commuting triangles, but everything follows from the one first simple diagram.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric"}> Eric
            \: Hi Toby (and anyone else who may stop by). Note the
            %strong Crash!
            above. The definition cannot be correct as stated. I probably won’t give up though. I think instead of saying the diagram commutes, I need to say something about “commuting up to a 2-morphism” or “there is a 2-morphism such that” blah blah.
